<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="50641" href="schedule/2020/spring/CMN/396/50641.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2020" href="schedule/2020.xml">2020</calendarYear><term id="120201" href="schedule/2020/spring.xml">Spring 2020</term><subject id="CMN" href="schedule/2020/spring/CMN.xml">Communication</subject><course id="396" href="schedule/2020/spring/CMN/396.xml">Special Topics in Comm</course></parents><sectionNumber>E  </sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Performing Rhetoric</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>This class examines the performance of rhetoric from historical and performance perspectives. After looking at the cultural-political-historical context of various ages of great oratory, the class addresses the rhetorical performance style of these ages. Throughout the course, students will use performance techniques of the various ages to perform a literary piece from that age.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2020-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2020-05-06Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="S5 ">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>12:30 PM</start><end>01:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>TR     </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>137</roomNumber><buildingName>Armory</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Gailey" firstName="B">Gailey, B</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>